      "title": "Unilateral and conditional",
      "narration": "A life policy is usually described as unilateral because the insurer makes the enforceable promise to pay covered benefits. The owner can generally stop paying future premiums, subject to policy consequences such as lapse or use of available values. This does not mean the policy has no conditions. Coverage may depend on required premiums, truthful application information and other valid terms. Unilateral identifies the contractual promise. Conditional reminds you that the promise operates under specified requirements. Do not mistake either word for an unconditional promise to pay every claim.",
